\subsection*{A4. Role of the LLM branch}

\reviewcomment{
The manuscript argues that LLM-only adaptation performs poorly on
regression-based time-series forecasting, yet RAN-NFM-Pro, which
incorporates an LLM branch, consistently outperforms RAN-NFM-Core.
The role of the LLM branch should be clarified.
}

\response{
We agree that the original presentation did not clearly distinguish
LLM-only prediction from the role of the LLM within RAN-NFM-Pro.
The LLM branch in Pro does not replace the RAN-specific representations
learned from structured operational data. Instead, it provides
complementary semantic and contextual representations that are fused
with the RAN-specific representation.

Our results therefore suggest that, for the RAN tasks considered in
this work, LLM-derived representations are more effective as
complementary features than as the sole representation for quantitative
prediction. We also clarify the Core--Pro tradeoff: Core targets
efficiency- and resource-sensitive settings, whereas Pro targets
accuracy-first settings where additional computation and memory
overhead are acceptable.
}

